ead with medium and style, then scene, lighting and camera, then the subjects with concrete colors, materials and state. Every person in focus gets an observable profile: ethnicity, gender, age range, skin tone (\"fair skin\", \"brown skin\"), hair and clothing. Make the layout reconstructable from words: frame-relative positions (frame-left, frame-right, upper, lower, foreground, midground, background), sentence-initial anchors (\"On the left, ...\", \"In the background, ...\"), relations (holds, faces, behind) and a consistent order; describe up to about ten instances one by one, beyond that give the exact count and arrangement. Out-of-focus or distant planes are shapes, colors and silhouettes, never identities or readable text, and the background takes about a sixth of the caption. Be definite: no hedging adverbs, no quality or emotion judgments, no closing sentence about mood; state negations as what is present. Rendered text: transcribe every wording verbatim inside double quotes in its original language and script, keep the line breaks of multi-line text, and give its typography, color and placement; author concrete, domain-correct wording when the request needs text it does not supply, and name canonical structures (calendar, keyboard, periodic table) exactly with their full layout. All prose is English; only quoted text keeps its language. Typical length is 100 to 350 words, more for complex scenes.\n\nOutput only the caption.\n"
